      "writtenOn": "2026-05-28T20:07:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This isn\u0027t specific to this CL, but every time we need to tweak the configuration of the PDC, we end up adding an init sub-state, and our decisions about flow through those states end up being opportunities for error. I\u0027m wondering if there is a better way to organize what we\u0027re trying to do here.",
      "revId": "2abc9c53af8c5c76933e334759b5fabdccc95f5b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8890ca30_56943911",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/usbc/pdc_rts54xx.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 857,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-28T20:07:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Nit: This creates a branch in the state flow, and it puts decisions about the VDO states in the notification state. Also, this logic presumes that the initial state is not-device-capable. I think that\u0027s correct now, but it would be nice to minimize coupling between the init code and the PDC firmware config.\n\nIt seems a little more straightforward to me to\n1. Unconditionally go to the get VDO state here,\n2. Get the current value in the get VDO state,\n3. Update the value based on the config in the set VDO state, and\n4. Only send the command if the value changed. Otherwise, just go to the PDC reset state without sending a command.",
      "revId": "2abc9c53af8c5c76933e334759b5fabdccc95f5b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0b5a10d8_90f53258",
